   150	
   151	
   152	_**Observation on NPE Detection.**_ We observed that state-of
   153	the-art tools, Randoop and EvoSuite, are not sufficiently effective
   154	at catching diverse NPEs in practice. These unit testing techniques
   155	primarily strive for high code coverage with the expectation that
   156	obtaining high code coverage will ultimately lead to bug detection. Unfortunately, however, we experienced that achieving higher
   157	code coverage does not necessarily result in better NPE-finding
   158	performance; using a highly tuned configuration for EvoSuite to
   159	increase line coverage from 64.5% to 77.8% on our benchmark programs ended up improving its NPE-reproduction rate marginally
   160	from 55.7% 56.9% (Section 4.3). This is because software bugs, especially NPEs, usually occur under certain conditions [ 18 – 20 ]. For
   161	example, NPEs are detected only if the value of the dereferenced
   162	pointer remains null at the time the corresponding buggy statement is executed. Hence, detecting NPEs requires taking one more
   163	step from just achieving high code coverage.
   164	
   165	
   166	_**Our Approach.**_ To take unit testing techniques one step further
   167	for effective NPE detection, we propose a new strategy that guides
   168	the testing process to generate test cases that are more likely to
   169	explore NPE-prone regions, where NPE may occur. To this end, our
   170	approach leverages both static and dynamic analyses. We use static
   171	analysis to identify the methods that may trigger NPEs and compute
   172	backward dependencies to pinpoint the statements and variables
   173	that need to be mutated to reach those methods. We utilize dynamic
   174	analysis to gather runtime information and adaptively spend more
   175	testing budget on less explored NPE-related code regions.
   176	Experiments show that our approach significantly improves the
   177	existing unit test generator in terms of NPE detection. We implemented our approach on top of EvoSuite, and evaluated its
   178	performance on 108 known NPEs collected from 96 real-world
   179	projects [ 3, 21 – 24 ] in comparison with Randoop and EvoSuite. In
   180	summary, NpeTest showed a reproduction rate of 78.9% for those
   181	NPEs while Randoop and EvoSuite achieved 33.7% and 56.9%, respectively. Regarding the number of detected bugs, 12 bugs were
   182	exclusively found by NpeTest, 1 by EvoSuite, and 0 by Randoop.
   183	We also conducted an industrial case study, applying the three
   184	tools, Randoop, EvoSuite, and NpeTest, to a software project
   185	actively used and maintained within a large IT company. Through
   186	the case study, we found a total of 91 previously unknown NPEs:
   187	NpeTest detected 89 NPEs, whereas EvoSuite and Randoop detected 82 and 52 NPEs, respectively. This case study prompted the
   188	development team to plan incorporating NpeTest into their development pipeline, enhancing overall code security and robustness.
   189	
   190	
   191	_**Contributions.**_ We summarize our contributions below:
   192	
   193	
   194	- **Observation** : We show that increasing code coverage does not
   195	always lead to better NPE detection. Using highly tuned EvoSuite did not meaningfully improve NPE-reproduction rates.
   196	
   197	- **New Technique** : We present NpeTest, a new NPE-detection
   198	strategy for EvoSuite . To our knowledge, NpeTest is the first
   199	technique for unit test generators that focuses on NPE detection.
   200	
   201	
   202	
   203	
   204	- **Evaluation and Comparison** : We demonstrate the effectiveness of NpeTest by evaluating it on 108 real-world NPEs and
   205	comparing it with Randoop and EvoSuite.
   206	
   207	- **Industrial Case Study** : We show that NpeTest can be useful
   208	in industrial contexts by conducting a case study on a software
   209	project actively maintained within a major IT company.
